Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• "event_type": filter only hail events (value ILIKE '%hail%').  
• "event_begin_time": restrict to the last 10 years (roughly 2014‑01‑01 → current).  
• "event_latitude" / "event_longitude": build a GEOGRAPHY/GEOMETRY point for every storm event.  
• "event_point": ready‑made point geometry (can be used directly with ST_WITHIN).  
• "state_fips_code": optional sanity check (events are U.S.‑only).  
• "zip_code_geom": polygon geometry for every ZIP code; used in ST_WITHIN() spatial join.  
• "zip_code": grouping key to count hail events per ZIP code.  
• "state_code", "city", "county": optional descriptive attributes for reporting.  

Typical conditions you’ll need  
• "event_type" ILIKE '%hail%'  
• "event_begin_time" >= DATE_PART(epoch_msec, '2014‑01‑01')   (if epoch_msec is confirmed)  
• "event_latitude" IS NOT NULL AND "event_longitude" IS NOT NULL  
• ST_WITHIN( ST_MAKEPOINT("event_longitude","event_latitude") , "z"."zip_code_geom")  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (each returns ≤ 20 rows) that gradually build the logic needed to answer “Which five ZIP codes have the most hail events in the past 10 years?”  
(Replace <TODAY_EPOCH_MSEC> with the current epoch‑milliseconds value if you want an exact 10‑year window.)

```sql
--Query 1 --------------------------------------------------------------
--Description: Inspect all distinct severe‑storm event types to confirm
--the exact spelling/letter‑case of the hail category for a recent year.
SELECT DISTINCT "event_type"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2024"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Query 2 --------------------------------------------------------------
--Description: Verify that ready‑made "event_point" geometry exists and
--check whether lat/lon fields are simultaneously populated.
SELECT "event_id",
       "event_type",
       "event_latitude",
       "event_longitude",
       "event_point"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2024"
WHERE "event_type" ILIKE '%hail%'
  AND "event_latitude" IS NOT NULL
  AND "event_longitude" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Query 3 --------------------------------------------------------------
--Description: Quick frequency count of hail events in one recent table.
SELECT COUNT(*) AS "hail_events_2023"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2023"
WHERE "event_type" ILIKE '%hail%';
```

```sql
--Query 4 --------------------------------------------------------------
--Description: Sample spatial join for 2024 hail events and ZIP codes
--(returns 20 hail events that fall inside a ZIP polygon).
SELECT s."event_id",
       z."zip_code",
       s."event_latitude",
       s."event_longitude"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2024"  s
JOIN "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."GEO_US_BOUNDARIES"."ZIP_CODES"              z
  ON ST_WITHIN(
        ST_MAKEPOINT(s."event_longitude", s."event_latitude"),
        z."zip_code_geom"
     )
WHERE s."event_type" ILIKE '%hail%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Query 5 --------------------------------------------------------------
--Description: Hail‑event counts per ZIP code for calendar year 2024.
SELECT z."zip_code",
       COUNT(*) AS "hail_events_2024"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2024"  s
JOIN "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."GEO_US_BOUNDARIES"."ZIP_CODES"              z
  ON ST_WITHIN(
        ST_MAKEPOINT(s."event_longitude", s."event_latitude"),
        z."zip_code_geom"
     )
WHERE s."event_type" ILIKE '%hail%'
GROUP BY z."zip_code"
ORDER BY "hail_events_2024" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Query 6 --------------------------------------------------------------
--Description: Combine two recent tables (2023 & 2024) to be sure UNION
--logic works and that column lists match.
SELECT "event_id",
       "event_type",
       "event_latitude",
       "event_longitude",
       2023 AS "yr"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2023"
WHERE "event_type" ILIKE '%hail%'
  AND "event_latitude" IS NOT NULL
  AND "event_longitude" IS NOT NULL
UNION ALL
SELECT "event_id",
       "event_type",
       "event_latitude",
       "event_longitude",
       2024 AS "yr"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2024"
WHERE "event_type" ILIKE '%hail%'
  AND "event_latitude" IS NOT NULL
  AND "event_longitude" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Query 7 --------------------------------------------------------------
--Description: Count hail events per ZIP code for a multi‑year period
--(2019‑2024) to test a rolling‑window calculation.
WITH hail AS (
  SELECT "event_id",
         ST_MAKEPOINT("event_longitude","event_latitude") AS "pt"
  FROM (
        SELECT * FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2019"
        UNION ALL
        SELECT * FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2020"
        UNION ALL
        SELECT * FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2021"
        UNION ALL
        SELECT * FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2022"
        UNION ALL
        SELECT * FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2023"
        UNION ALL
        SELECT * FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2024"
       )
  WHERE "event_type" ILIKE '%hail%'
    AND "event_latitude" IS NOT NULL
    AND "event_longitude" IS NOT NULL
)
SELECT z."zip_code",
       COUNT(*) AS "hail_events_6yr"
FROM hail h
JOIN "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."GEO_US_BOUNDARIES"."ZIP_CODES" z
  ON ST_WITHIN(h."pt", z."zip_code_geom")
GROUP BY z."zip_code"
ORDER BY "hail_events_6yr" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

