Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• "event_type": filter only hail events (value ILIKE '%hail%').  
• "event_begin_time": restrict to the last 10 years (roughly 2014‑01‑01 → current).  
• "event_latitude" / "event_longitude": build a GEOGRAPHY/GEOMETRY point for every storm event.  
• "event_point": ready‑made point geometry (can be used directly with ST_WITHIN).  
• "state_fips_code": optional sanity check (events are U.S.‑only).  
• "zip_code_geom": polygon geometry for every ZIP code; used in ST_WITHIN() spatial join.  
• "zip_code": grouping key to count hail events per ZIP code.  
• "state_code", "city", "county": optional descriptive attributes for reporting.  

Typical conditions you’ll need  
• "event_type" ILIKE '%hail%'  
• "event_begin_time" >= DATE_PART(epoch_msec, '2014‑01‑01')   (if epoch_msec is confirmed)  
• "event_latitude" IS NOT NULL AND "event_longitude" IS NOT NULL  
• ST_WITHIN( ST_MAKEPOINT("event_longitude","event_latitude") , "z"."zip_code_geom")  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (each returns ≤ 20 rows) that gradually build the logic needed to answer “Which five ZIP codes have the most hail events in the past 10 years?”  
(Replace <TODAY_EPOCH_MSEC> with the current epoch‑milliseconds value if you want an exact 10‑year window.)

```sql
--Query 1 --------------------------------------------------------------
--Description: Inspect all distinct severe‑storm event types to confirm
--the exact spelling/letter‑case of the hail category for a recent year.
SELECT DISTINCT "event_type"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS"."STORMS_2024"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
